# Deploy-safety FLOOR — per-gate fail-policy primitive (Sub-cycle B) — apply ladder

Per `docs/plans/deploy-safety-floor-gate-primitives.md` §6 Sub-cycle B —
generalizes the fail-CLOSED gate-policy convention into a reusable,
per-gate FIELD in the safety-layer (the home of `D-SECHK.FAIL-OPEN`),
closing G15 for the floor without inverting the advisory convention
everywhere (plan Decision C).

Scope (plan §6 Sub-cycle B): EXTEND the sealed `framework/safety-layer/`.

  - AC.DSF.5 — per-gate fail-policy primitive. New stdlib-only sibling
    module `hooks/_fail_policy.py`: `FailPolicy{FAIL_OPEN, FAIL_CLOSED}`,
    `DEFAULT_FAIL_POLICY = FAIL_OPEN`, `resolve_fault` (pure) +
    `apply_fault_policy` (resolve-and-enact). The four advisory guards
    (`dangerous_flag_guard`, `secret_pattern_guard`, `config_write_guard`,
    `wd_discipline_guard`) DECLARE `FAIL_POLICY = FailPolicy.FAIL_OPEN` and
    route their top-level fault handler through the primitive —
    behavior-preserving (FAIL_OPEN emits nothing, exit 0, identical log
    label), so no existing sealed advisory guard regresses. Only floor
    destructive gates opt into FAIL_CLOSED; the convention is NOT
    blanket-flipped.

Keystone (plan §6 entry precondition; §8 halt) — EMPIRICALLY VERIFIED at
build time, artifact-probed (not self-reported): a PreToolUse
`permissionDecision: deny` IS honored as a tool-call block under Claude
Code bypass-all modes — both `--permission-mode bypassPermissions` AND
`--dangerously-skip-permissions`, in an isolated temp project with an
MCP-isolated `claude -p`. The sentinel command did NOT execute under either
mode. POSITIVE => the floor's fail-CLOSED destructive guarantee is
reachable framework-side; the build proceeded. This closes the exact
question Sub-cycle A's `test_real_entrypoint_bypass_mode_still_returns_deny`
docstring deferred to Sub-cycle B.

Tests (AC.DSF.5), all at the real entry-point:
  - `test_AC_DSF_5_fail_policy_primitive` — policy resolution (default
    FAIL_OPEN; FAIL_CLOSED+candidate => deny; FAIL_CLOSED+non-candidate =>
    allow, read parity), the PreToolUse deny contract, and
    `apply_fault_policy` enactment (emits deny only when closed+candidate).
  - `test_AC_DSF_5_advisory_guards_fail_open` — (b) regression: each
    advisory guard DECLARES FAIL_OPEN and still fails open at the real
    subprocess entry-point under non-JSON / malformed input.
  - `test_AC_DSF_5_outcome_altitude` ★ — outcome-altitude: the primitive
    writes the honored PreToolUse deny envelope on a FAIL_CLOSED fault with
    no pre-arranged state; cross-checks a REAL floor PreToolUse hook
    (`deploy_safety_floor_guard`) denying on fault under an envelope
    `permission_mode` of `bypassPermissions` (skip-if-absent, so the
    safety-layer suite stays self-contained).

Lens 1 (compose, don't re-derive): the primitive mirrors the proven
stdlib-only sibling-module pattern (`_secret_patterns.py`) so a bare-script
hook imports it without pulling the `loam.safety_layer` package (and its
heavier deps) at hook runtime — preserving the advisory guards'
robustness-by-stdlib invariant.

Method-level choices are the builder's call per ODD §1.1; the plan fixed
the AC (§5), Decision C (per-gate field, default fail-open), the fence
(§2/§6 — safety-layer extend), and the halt triggers (§8), not the
implementation.

F2 / scope boundary (surfaced to dispatcher): the deploy-safety-floor gate
already fails CLOSED ad-hoc (Sub-cycle A) and is NOT touched here; adopting
this shared primitive there is a named follow-up. The harness-level hook
process-KILL timeout is a Claude Code platform behavior, not catchable
in-process — the primitive covers the catchable in-process faults (raise /
malformed input / a gate's own internal-budget timeout routed through it).

